## Components in React: functions that return JSX
A React **component** is a JavaScript function that takes **props** (its inputs) and returns **JSX** (a description of UI that looks like HTML):
```jsx
function MenuItem({ name, price, description }) {   // props come in
  return (                                          // JSX comes out
    <div className="menu-item">
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>{description}</p>
      <span>${price}</span>
    </div>
  );
}
// use it:
<MenuItem name="Latte" price={4.5} description="Espresso and steamed milk" />
```
This is *exactly* the component model from Astro, a reusable, prop-driven piece of UI, now in React. JSX is HTML-like syntax inside JavaScript: it lets you write the UI structure naturally while embedding dynamic values and expressions with `{ }` (`{name}`, `{price * 1.1}`, `{items.map(...)}`), the same `{ }` you used in Astro. React calls your function, takes the returned JSX, and renders it to the DOM.

## Composition: building UIs from component trees
The power of components is **composition**, building complex UIs by nesting components into a *tree*, which is the mental model React is named for:
```jsx
function Menu({ items }) {
  return (
    <section>
      <h2>Our Menu</h2>
      {items.map(item => <MenuItem key={item.id} {...item} />)}   // a list of components
    </section>
  );
}
function App() {
  return (<><Header /><Menu items={menu} /><Footer /></>);        // composed from pieces
}
```
Your whole UI becomes a tree: `App` contains `Header`, `Menu`, `Footer`; `Menu` contains many `MenuItem`s; and so on. This composition is the heart of the component mental model, you *decompose* a complex interface into small, focused, reusable components, each with one clear job, then *compose* them into the full UI, building complexity from simple, understandable pieces. Rendering a list (a common need) is just mapping data to components (`items.map(item => <MenuItem ... />)`), with a `key` prop on each (a unique id React uses to track list items efficiently, forgetting it is a classic beginner warning). Thinking in component trees, "this UI is a `Page` made of a `Header`, a `Sidebar`, and a `Content` made of `Card`s", is the skill that makes building any interface manageable.

## Props flow down: one-way data flow
A crucial principle of how React components relate: **data flows one way, down the tree, via props.** A parent passes data to its children through props, children receive and use that data, but a child cannot reach up and change its parent's data directly, data goes *down*, not up. This **one-way data flow** is deliberate and is what makes React apps predictable: you can always trace where a piece of data comes from (it was passed as a prop from above), so understanding why a component shows what it shows is a matter of following the props down from their source, rather than hunting through scattered code that might have changed it. It contrasts with the tangled two-way bindings of older approaches, where data could change from anywhere and tracking it down was a nightmare. (When a child *does* need to affect a parent, the parent passes down a *function* prop that the child calls, so even "upward" communication is just a prop flowing down, keeping the one-way model intact, you will use this constantly.) Understanding that props flow down and form a one-way data flow is key to reasoning about React: the UI is a tree, data descends through it as props, and that predictable flow is what keeps even large component trees comprehensible.

## Where this fits
You can build React components, functions taking props and returning JSX, and compose them into trees (decompose the UI into focused pieces, compose them into the whole), with data flowing one way down via props for predictability. This is the component mental model, now interactive. Tomorrow you add the thing that makes components come alive: state.

## Forms in React: controlled inputs
Forms are everywhere, and React handles them with **controlled components**, where the input's value is driven by state. Instead of the DOM holding the input's value (as in weeks 1-3), React state is the *single source of truth*: the input shows the state, and typing updates the state:
```jsx
function ContactForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [message, setMessage] = useState('');
  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();                       // stop the native page reload
    // ...do something with email and message
  }
  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} />
      <textarea value={message} onChange={e => setMessage(e.target.value)} />
      <button>Send</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```
Each input's `value` comes from state, and its `onChange` updates that state on every keystroke, so the state *always* mirrors what is in the input. This is the declarative model applied to forms: the form's data lives in state, and the inputs are just a reflection of it.

## Why controlled inputs, and the trade-off
Controlled inputs follow directly from React's "UI as a function of state" philosophy, and the payoff is that **your component always knows the form's current values** (they are in state), which makes everything else easy: live validation (check the email as they type), enabling/disabling the submit button based on the values, transforming input (uppercase, formatting), and using the values on submit, all just read from state. The form's data and the form's display are guaranteed to be in sync because the state *is* the data and the inputs *render* it. The trade-off is some verbosity (a piece of state and an onChange per field), which for large forms is why form libraries (React Hook Form, W8) exist to reduce the boilerplate, but understanding the controlled-component model first is essential, because the libraries are built on it. There is also an *uncontrolled* approach (let the DOM hold the value, read it via a ref on submit, the W4-D4 ref idea), which is simpler for trivial forms and sometimes used, but controlled inputs are the React default because they give you the live, always-in-sync access to form values that interactive forms need. Knowing both, and that controlled is the standard, lets you choose appropriately.

## The submit handler and preventing default
A detail that trips up everyone and teaches a real lesson: the `e.preventDefault()` in the submit handler. By default, submitting a form triggers the *native* browser submission, the full-page reload to the form's `action` URL from week 3, and in a React single-page app you almost never want that (it would reload the whole app and discard your state). So you call `e.preventDefault()` to *stop* the native submission and handle the data yourself in JavaScript (validate it, send it via an API call, update state). This is a perfect callback to the native form model you learned in week 3: React forms are *built on top of* native forms, and `preventDefault` is you explicitly opting *out* of the native submit-and-reload behaviour to take control. Understanding this connects your React knowledge to the fundamentals, the native HTTP form submission is still underneath, React just intercepts it, which is exactly why learning the native model first (week 3) pays off now. Forgetting `preventDefault` causes the baffling "my form reloads the page and loses everything" bug, and knowing *why* (the native submission is firing) lets you fix it instantly. Controlled inputs plus a `preventDefault`ing submit handler is the standard React form pattern, and it rests on the form fundamentals you already have.

## Where this fits
You can build React forms with controlled components (state as the single source of truth, inputs reflect and update it) and a submit handler that `preventDefault`s the native submission to take control, the declarative model applied to forms, built on the native form fundamentals from week 3. Tomorrow you learn to keep React fast as components grow: rendering performance.

## The core distinction: server vs client components
The single most important thing to understand in the App Router is **when a component runs on the server versus the client**, because it determines what the component can do. By default, components are **server components**, but you opt into **client components** with the `"use client"` directive when you need browser-only features:
```jsx
// server component (default): runs on the server
async function MenuPage() {
  const items = await db.menu.findMany();    // direct database access - server only!
  return <Menu items={items} />;             // sends rendered HTML
}

// client component: runs in the browser
"use client";
function FilterButton() {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(false);  // useState, onClick - need the browser
  return <button onClick={() => setActive(!active)}>...</button>;
}
```
The rule: **server components for fetching data and rendering; client components for interactivity.**

## What each can and cannot do
Knowing the capabilities and limits of each is what lets you place the boundary correctly:
- **Server components** (the default): can directly access databases, the filesystem, and secrets (env vars), can be `async` to fetch data, and send HTML to the browser, but *cannot* use state (`useState`), effects, event handlers (`onClick`), or any browser API, because they do not run in the browser. They are for the *static, data-driven* parts of your UI.
- **Client components** (`"use client"`): can use state, effects, event handlers, and browser APIs (everything from last week's React), but run in the browser, so they *cannot* directly access the database or secrets (that code would be exposed). They are for the *interactive* parts.
So you cannot use `onClick` or `useState` in a server component (you get an error reminding you to add `"use client"`), and you should not try to query the database in a client component (the connection would be exposed). Each kind is suited to a different job, and the error messages guide you, "you're using state, this needs to be a client component."

## The mental model: a tree of mostly-server, islands-of-client
The architecture that makes this powerful, and the mental model to internalise, is that your app is a **tree of mostly server components with islands of client components** where interactivity is needed. You make components server by default (so most of your app benefits from server rendering, fast, SEO-friendly, secure data access) and mark *only* the genuinely interactive pieces as client components, keeping those islands as small as possible. For example, a product page is a server component (it fetches and renders the product data on the server), but the "add to cart" button inside it is a small client component (it needs `onClick`). This minimises the JavaScript sent to the browser (only the client islands ship their code) while keeping the data-fetching and rendering on the server. The discipline is to *default to server* and *reach for client only when you need interactivity* (state, events, browser APIs), pushing the `"use client"` boundary as far down the tree as possible so the interactive part is a small leaf, not a whole branch. Getting this boundary right, server for data and structure, small client islands for interactivity, is the central skill of the App Router, and it is what delivers the performance and SEO benefits while still allowing rich interactivity. Mis-placing it (making everything a client component, or trying to fetch data in a client component) loses the benefits, so understanding *which runs where and why* is the foundational App Router skill.

## Where this fits
You understand server vs client components: server by default (data access, secrets, HTML output, no interactivity) and client via `"use client"` (interactivity, browser APIs, no direct DB), with the model of a mostly-server tree with small client islands placed only where interactivity is needed. This is the central App Router distinction. Tomorrow you handle dynamic routes, pages whose content depends on the URL.

## Server actions: mutations without building an API
So far you have *fetched* and *displayed* data; **server actions** let you *change* it, submit a form, create a record, update something, by calling a server-side function *directly* from your component, with no manual API endpoint to build. You mark a function with `"use server"`, and it runs on the server when called (e.g. on form submit):
```jsx
async function createMessage(formData) {
  "use server";                              // this runs on the SERVER
  const message = formData.get('message');
  await db.messages.create({ data: { message } });   // direct database write
}
function ContactForm() {
  return (
    <form action={createMessage}>            // the form calls the server action
      <textarea name="message" />
      <button>Send</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```
When the form submits, `createMessage` runs *on the server* (with database access, secrets, all server capabilities), no fetch call, no API route, you just wrote a server function and wired the form to it.

## Why server actions are a big simplification
Server actions collapse a traditionally multi-step process into one, and understanding what they replace shows their value. The classic way to handle a form submission in a React app: write a client-side handler that `fetch`es a backend API endpoint, write that API endpoint (a separate server route) to receive the request, validate it, and write to the database, and wire the two together with the right URL, method, and JSON. Server actions eliminate most of that boilerplate: you write *one* server function and attach it to the form, and Next.js handles the wiring (the form posts to the action, the function runs on the server). This is a genuine simplification of the most common app operation, *mutating data in response to a user action*, and it keeps the logic where it belongs (the database write is in a server function with server access) without forcing you to build and maintain a separate API layer for every mutation. It also integrates with the React model: a server action can trigger a re-fetch/re-render so the UI updates after the mutation (the new message appears), giving you the full "user does something -> data changes -> UI reflects it" loop with minimal code. For the common case, a form that creates or updates data, server actions are the cleanest path.

## When server actions, and when a real API
The judgement to develop is *when* server actions fit and when you still want a traditional API endpoint (tomorrow's route handlers), because both have their place. **Server actions** are ideal for *your own app's* mutations triggered from *your own* UI, form submissions, button-triggered changes, the internal data operations of your application, where the tight integration and reduced boilerplate are exactly what you want. **Route handlers / a real API** (tomorrow) are what you need when something *other than your own React UI* must call your server: a mobile app, a third-party integration, a webhook, a public API for other developers, anything that needs a standard HTTP endpoint with a stable URL and contract. So the rule of thumb: server actions for your app's internal mutations (the common case, less boilerplate), a route handler/API for endpoints that external clients consume. This connects forward to the Backend phase (W11, REST API design): server actions are a Next.js convenience for internal mutations, while real API design (proper endpoints, methods, status codes) is for when you are exposing functionality beyond your own UI. Knowing which to reach for, the integrated server action for internal app mutations, the standalone API for external consumers, is the architectural call, and it keeps you from either over-building APIs for simple internal forms or trying to force server actions to serve external clients. For Bean Forge's own forms and data changes, server actions are the clean, modern choice.

## Where this fits
You understand server actions: server-side functions (`"use server"`) called directly from your components to mutate data, eliminating the API-endpoint boilerplate for your app's *internal* mutations (forms, updates), while real API route handlers serve *external* consumers. Tomorrow you build those traditional API endpoints with route handlers, for when you need a real HTTP API.

## Streaming and Suspense: fast even when data is slow
Server components fetch data on the server, but what if some data is *slow* (a slow database query, an external API)? Without help, the whole page would wait for the slowest part before *anything* shows, a blank screen while one slow query runs. **Streaming** with **Suspense** fixes this: the page sends the fast parts immediately and *streams in* the slow parts as they become ready, so the user sees content right away. You wrap a slow part in `<Suspense>` with a fallback (a loading placeholder):
```jsx
function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <Header />                              {/* fast: shows immediately */}
      <Suspense fallback={<MenuSkeleton />}>
        <Menu />                              {/* slow data: streams in when ready */}
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}
```
The header and layout appear instantly with a skeleton placeholder where the menu will be, and when the menu's data is ready, it streams in and replaces the skeleton, no blank-screen wait.

## Why streaming improves the real experience
Streaming addresses the gap between *total load time* and *perceived speed*, the same distinction as time-to-first-token in the AI track, and it is a major UX win. Without streaming, the user stares at nothing until the *slowest* piece of data is ready, so one slow query makes the *entire* page feel slow even if everything else was instant. With streaming, the page's structure and fast content appear immediately (the user sees *something* useful right away), and the slow parts fill in progressively with clear loading placeholders, so the page *feels* fast even though the slow data takes the same total time to arrive. This matters because perceived performance drives user satisfaction: a page that shows its header, nav, and fast content in 200ms and streams in a slow section over the next second feels far better than one that shows nothing for that whole second and then everything at once. Suspense's fallback (the skeleton/loading state) is key, it tells the user "this part is coming," turning a blank wait into a clear, progressive load. Streaming lets you build pages that are resilient to slow data: one slow component no longer holds the whole page hostage, it just streams in late while everything else is already usable.

## The mental model: progressive, prioritised loading
The deeper idea streaming teaches is **progressive, prioritised rendering**, sending the page in order of importance and readiness rather than all-or-nothing, which is a powerful way to think about loading any complex UI. You decide what should appear first (the layout, the critical content, the fast stuff) and let the slower, less critical parts stream in afterward, each wrapped in Suspense so it loads independently with its own placeholder. This means a complex page with several data sources of varying speed is no longer bottlenecked by its slowest source: the page is composed of independently-streaming sections, each appearing as soon as *it* is ready, in priority order. The skill is identifying which parts of a page are fast vs slow and wrapping the slow ones in Suspense boundaries so they do not block the rest, giving the user the fastest possible meaningful first view. This is the production-grade approach to data-driven pages, and it composes beautifully with server components: the server renders and streams the fast parts immediately while the slow data is still being fetched, then streams those in. Understanding streaming and Suspense, send the fast and important content first, stream the slow content in progressively with loading placeholders, completes your picture of how Next.js delivers fast, resilient, production-quality pages even when the underlying data is not uniformly fast. It is the difference between a page that is only as fast as its slowest query and one that feels fast regardless.

## Where this fits
